  <data name="InfoBroker" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Changes here take effect on Apply, which remakes the connection once. Anything left on Automatic is found by trying the broker when Test connection or Apply is pressed, and at no other time.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="InfoBrokerInUse" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>The address the live connection is using — the saved settings, not any unapplied edit in the fields below.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="InfoConnection" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Where the broker settings in force came from, and whether the live link is encrypted — a setting left on Automatic can land on either.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="InfoDevice" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>How this machine appears to anything reading its entities: a display name, and the identifier every topic is built from.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="InfoDeviceId" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Lower-case letters, digits and underscores only, up to {0} characters. An empty value falls back to the name derived from this machine.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="InfoDeviceName" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Cosmetic — it renames nothing else. An empty box falls back to the name derived from this machine, shown as the placeholder. Saved when the box is left, not on Apply.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="InfoDiscoveryPrefix" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Entities are announced using the Home Assistant MQTT Discovery convention, an openly published specification that some MQTT consumers follow and others ignore. Change the prefix only for a consumer that listens elsewhere.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="InfoEncryption" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Automatic tries each endpoint encrypted first, and falls back to clear text only where the broker offers no encryption at all — a rejected password or an untrusted certificate ends the search instead. A choice made by hand is always used, and a host typed as a wss:// address is encrypted whatever this is set to.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="InfoHost" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>A name or an address. A full ws:// or wss:// address is used exactly as typed, including any path.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="InfoLastCommand" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>The most recent command the broker sent that was acted on. One that was refused never appears here.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="InfoLastPublish" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>When a message last reached the broker. Unchanged values are not re-sent, so this stands still while nothing changes. Publish now sends the current state for the groups switched on below.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="InfoPassword" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Never written to the log, and never published.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="InfoPort" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Automatic tries the ports brokers commonly use, encrypted and plain, and remembers the one that answered. Choose Other… to type a port.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="InfoPublishSwitch" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>The master switch for the whole feature. Nothing on the network is touched while it is off, and it takes effect immediately.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="InfoStatus" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Read-only, and about the live connection rather than the fields below.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="InfoTest" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Tries the values in the fields above, including ones not yet applied, over a throwaway connection of its own. It saves nothing at all: not the fields, and not where the broker answered. Anything left on Automatic may take several addresses to find; the result names the one that answered, and lists what was tried only when none of them did.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="InfoTransport" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Automatic tries TCP first, then WebSocket, and remembers whichever answered — useful for a machine that is on the internal network sometimes and outside it at others. A transport chosen by hand is always used.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="InfoUsername" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Changing the username starts the search for the broker endpoint again: a broker often serves a separate listener per account.</value>
  </data>
